# The M1 gate, as a re-runnable script.
#
#   python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory web/dist-m1 &
#   node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --out /tmp/gate-chrome \
#        --script-file web/tools/menu-gate.steps
#   node web/tools/drive-firefox.mjs          --out /tmp/gate-firefox \
#        --script-file web/tools/menu-gate.steps
#   kill %1
#
# --headed is REQUIRED for Chrome: headless Chrome 152 floods the page with
# thousands of spurious keydown events per real keypress, which drowns the
# SDL event queue. Firefox headless is fine. Both are explained in the header
# comment of the respective driver.
#
# PASS looks like: ten screenshots, all ten different, and a transcript with
# no "Stack overflow detected", no [EXCEPTION], and no "SDL event queue full".
# Every 404 in the transcript is /favicon.ico (the browser asks for it once per
# navigation and python3 -m http.server has none); any other 404 is a missing
# asset and is a failure. Both drivers now print the URL alongside each failed
# load, so that rule can actually be checked -- before, Chrome logged the
# status without the URL and Firefox logged no network events at all.
#
# AND, SINCE M6, ONE MORE LINE HAS TO SAY PASS: the [SPARKSGATE] D1 check below.
# It is this gate's half of a touch-only change -- see the section comment on it.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DEMONSTRATE. It demonstrates every menu input
# path M1 can reach: Down, Up, Left/Right, Enter and Escape, each with a
# before/after screenshot pair.
#
# It does NOT demonstrate entering a submenu and coming back out, and an
# earlier version of this file claimed it did -- shots 07/08 were labelled
# "submenu" and "submenu-down" when they were the same First Setup menu as 04
# and 06. The reason it does not is not laziness: **there is no submenu on the
# path M1 reaches.** Both menus reachable before gameplay were enumerated from
# the source, and neither contains a uMenuItemSubmenu (uMenu.h:468):
#
#   language menu (gLanguageMenu.cpp:125-141)  -- uMenuItemLanguage only
#   First Setup   (gArmagetron.cpp:152-226)    -- uMenuItemExit (Accept),
#       uMenuItemString (Name), and three uMenuItemSelection (Colour,
#       Controls, Connection). "Controls:" in particular LOOKS like a submenu
#       and is not: it is uMenuItemSelection<tString> (gArmagetron.cpp:175),
#       a left/right chooser over keys_*.cfg templates.
#
# The real main menu (gMenus.cpp, where the uMenuItemSubmenu items live) is
# only reached after welcome() runs a tutorial single-player game
# (gArmagetron.cpp:391), because st_FirstUse is true on every load until M4
# adds persistence. Driving through that is gameplay, which is M2's. Note for
# whoever picks this up: st_FirstUse is exposed as the config item FIRST_USE
# (tConfiguration.cpp:402), so "FIRST_USE 0" would skip straight to the main
# menu -- but that changes what every real user sees on boot, so it is a
# product decision, not a test fix.
#
# Reading the screenshots (a first run, so the client walks the first-use path;
# the menu lists items Accept / Name / Colour / Controls / Connection / Accept
# from the top):
#   01  language menu, "American English" selected
#   02  "British English" selected                    <- Down works
#   03  "First Setup" menu, cursor on "Accept"        <- Enter works: it chose
#                                                        a language AND left
#                                                        the language menu
#   04  cursor on "Name: web_user"                    <- Down
#   05  cursor on "Colour: ..."                       <- Down
#   06  the Colour VALUE has changed                  <- Right works
#   07  cursor on "Controls: ..."                     <- Down
#   08  the Controls VALUE has changed                <- Right, on a second
#                                                        item, so it is the
#                                                        key and not the item
#   09  cursor back on "Colour", both changed values still showing   <- Up
#   10  "Welcome to Armagetron Advanced!"             <- Escape leaves the
#                                                        First Setup menu
#
# The text cursor visible next to "Name:" in 04 is NOT an edit mode. Nothing in
# uMenu has one: uMenuItemString::Render (uMenu.cpp:811-830) draws the cursor
# whenever the item is selected, and uMenuItemString::Event takes keystrokes
# straight from the selected item. Pressing Enter on "Name:" does nothing, and
# an earlier version of this file read that as "entered the field".
#
# The exact menu contents will drift as the port progresses; what has to stay
# true is that every screenshot differs from the one before it.

wait:6000
eval:(()=>{const c=document.getElementById('canvas');return 'canvas '+c.width+'x'+c.height})()
until:1:120000:[BOOT] autostart

# ===========================================================================
# D1: THE DESKTOP AUTOEXEC IS THE ONE THE BUILD SHIPPED.
#
# M6 task 8 turns the crash sparks off ON TOUCH DEVICES ONLY -- they cost about
# a quarter of the frame while a cycle is actually sparking against a wall
# (17.15 ms median stock against 13.1 with SPARKS 0 in the arm that sparked in
# 29 of its 40 rim seconds; a second stock run that sparked in 11 of 40 read
# 14.0 against 13.65; docs/evidence/m6-lag/task8-sparks/), and a desktop is not
# the machine that is short of milliseconds. The page appends
# the setting to the preloaded /data/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg at runtime rather
# than the repo editing that file, which is what makes it conditional at all.
#
# So this gate owns the NEGATIVE half of that claim, and it owns it by reading
# the file rather than by trusting the branch: the page must say it wrote
# nothing, and the file the game parses must contain no SPARKS anywhere -- not
# the setting, not the comment the touch path writes above it. The positive
# half is T1b in web/tools/touch-gate.steps.
#
# It adds no screenshot on purpose: the PASS above is "ten screenshots, all ten
# different", and a check about a file has nothing to show.
# ===========================================================================
until:1:30000:[SPARKS] stock sparks, nothing written
eval:(()=>{const p='/data/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg';let t=null,err=null;try{t=Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){err=String(e)}const ok=typeof t==='string';const n=ok?(t.match(/SPARKS/g)||[]).length:-1;return '[SPARKSGATE] D1 desktop-autoexec-untouched '+JSON.stringify({read:ok,err:err,bytes:ok?t.length:0,sparks_occurrences:n,tail:ok?t.slice(-64):null,PASS:ok&&n===0})})()

wait:5000
mark:should-be-the-language-menu
shot:01-language-menu
key:Down
wait:1200
shot:02-language-menu-british-english-selected
mark:enter-chooses-a-language-and-leaves-this-menu
key:Enter
wait:3000
shot:03-first-setup-cursor-on-accept
# Is the frame limiter actually limiting? Every SwapGL() yields twice per frame
# under MAX_FPS > 0 -- emscripten_sleep(0) at the top, then sr_LimitFPS()'s
# emscripten_sleep(pad) -- and both are setTimeout underneath. So setTimeout
# calls per second is ~2x the frame rate. At MAX_FPS 60 expect ~120/s; with the
# limiter compiled out (as it was before this was fixed) emscripten_sleep(0)
# alone runs at the browser's ~4ms nested-timeout clamp, i.e. ~250/s.
eval:(()=>{window.__st=0;const o=window.setTimeout;window.setTimeout=function(){window.__st++;return o.apply(this,arguments)};window.__t0=performance.now();return 'setTimeout counter installed'})()
wait:3000
eval:(()=>{const dt=(performance.now()-window.__t0)/1000;return 'setTimeout calls/sec='+(window.__st/dt).toFixed(1)+' => ~'+(window.__st/dt/2).toFixed(1)+' FPS (MAX_FPS 60)'})()
key:Down
wait:1200
shot:04-first-setup-cursor-on-name
key:Down
wait:1200
shot:05-first-setup-cursor-on-colour
mark:left-right-changes-a-selection-value
key:Right
wait:1200
shot:06-first-setup-colour-value-changed
key:Down
wait:1200
shot:07-first-setup-cursor-on-controls
key:Right
wait:1200
shot:08-first-setup-controls-value-changed
key:Up
wait:1200
shot:09-first-setup-cursor-back-on-colour
mark:escape-leaves-the-first-setup-menu-entirely
key:Escape
wait:3000
shot:10-welcome-message
# The strings the game's own glGetString(GL_VENDOR/GL_RENDERER) return are the
# masked WebGL ones, not the unmasked driver ones -- see
# docs/porting/browser-runtime-notes.md section 6.
eval:(()=>{const c=document.getElementById('canvas');const g=c.getContext('webgl');const d=g.getExtension('WEBGL_debug_renderer_info');return JSON.stringify({VENDOR:g.getParameter(g.VENDOR),RENDERER:g.getParameter(g.RENDERER),VERSION:g.getParameter(g.VERSION),UNMASKED_VENDOR:d?g.getParameter(d.UNMASKED_VENDOR_WEBGL):null,UNMASKED_RENDERER:d?g.getParameter(d.UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL):null,preserveDrawingBuffer:g.getContextAttributes().preserveDrawingBuffer})})()
